      "title": "Chapter 5: Reconciling Everything Before You File",
      "lessons": [
        {
          "title": "Reconciling Form 16, Form 26AS and AIS Step by Step",
          "slug": "reconciling-form-16-form-26as-and-ais-step-by-step",
          "body": [
            {
              "_key": "k1",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "k2",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "You now know what each document is, how to read it and how to get it corrected. This lesson puts them on one table. Reconciliation is not an audit. It is a structured way of answering two questions before you file: have I reported every rupee of income the department knows about, and am I claiming every rupee of tax already paid for me? Done properly, it takes an evening and saves months of notices.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": []
            },
            {
              "_type": "bigIdea",
              "text": "Reconcile in both directions. Every entry in the department's documents should be in your return, and every claim in your return should be backed by the department's documents.",
              "_key": "k3"
            },
            {
              "_key": "k4",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "h2",
              "children": [
